<p>{% person "frederick-hager" %}Frederick Hager{% endperson %} and {% person "robert-hager" %}Robert Hager{% endperson %} of St. Paul opened the InterOcean Hotel across Main Street from the new Northern Pacific Railway depot. The Hager brothers had prospered through real-estate speculation and the lumber business.</p>

<p>While observing the March 1881 flood through binoculars from the bluffs east of the Missouri River, Frederick saw an opportunity. He took a train to St. Paul, visited the Northern Pacific Railway Land Office, and purchased half the lots in western Mandan. Frederick returned with railcars of lumber and his brothers Robert and {% person "joseph-hager" %}Joseph{% endperson %}, who joined him in the lumber business on West Main Street. The brothers bought properties for slightly more than $60 per lot and, after Northern Pacific located its passenger depot nearby, resold them within two years for $500 to $1,500 each.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/inter-ocean-hotel/708-1906-interocean-hotel-modified.jpg" alt="InterOcean Hotel in 1906">
    <figcaption>InterOcean Hotel, 1906</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Their hotel stood at the northwest corner of Dilworth Avenue—now Third Avenue Northwest—and Main Street. Built and furnished for {% inflation 86000, 1882 %}, it was Mandan's first three-story building and opened in April 1882. {% person "art-kredler" %}Art Kredler{% endperson %} was its first manager and remained in the position until failing health forced his resignation in February 1926.</p>

<p>The magnificent three-story brick hotel stood directly opposite the new {% inflation 12000, 1882 %} Northern Pacific passenger depot. Its opening coincided with completion of the railroad bridge across the Missouri River. The Hagers encouraged other investors to join Mandan's boom. {% person "marion-a-vinton" %}Marion A. Vinton{% endperson %} of New York and {% person "tilden-r-selmes" %}Tilden R. Selmes{% endperson %} of St. Paul helped finance brick stores along West Main Street.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/inter-ocean-hotel/388-interocean-line-sketch-1883-mandan-pioneer.jpg" alt="Line drawing of the InterOcean Hotel published in 1883">
  </figure>
</div>

<blockquote>
  <p>“The hotel is fitted up throughout in most exquisite taste and is absolutely perfect and complete in all its details. The management which has revolved upon Mr. {% person "john-hager" %}John Hager{% endperson %} has been fully in keeping with the house superlatively first class and the happy combination has made the ‘The Inter-Ocean’ the most powerful advertising medium of Mandan.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>—Advertisement in the <em>Daily Pioneer</em>, “Immigrant Edition No. 2,” 1883</p>

<p>Among the hotel's most notable features was indoor plumbing, then uncommon in Dakota hotels. It also had central steam heat supplied by an Osborne patented apparatus installed for {% inflation 12000, 1882 %}. Each guest could regulate the radiator in their room. The hotel's furnishings were compared with those in New York's finest hotels, and the management took pride in advertising a dining-room staff of “colored” waiters.</p>

<p>In 1883, a local newspaper reported that prominent guests including {% person "henry-villard" %}Henry Villard{% endperson %}, {% person "rufus-hatch" %}Rufus Hatch{% endperson %}, and {% person "john-rose" %}Sir John Rose{% endperson %} had called it the finest and most complete hotel west of Chicago. {% person "sitting-bull" %}Sitting Bull{% endperson %} attended a musical play there.</p>

